  "worked_solution": "First, we let: \n\n- $S$ be the position of Captain Sparrow's ship\n\n- $J$ be the position of Captain Jones' ship\n\n- $L$ be the position of the lighthouse\n\nLet's draw $△SJL.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242062/assets/daa3d50135304457e258e3f702a115162a085a4c3940070b4ac3cef77d5ae748.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nSL=150\\,\\text{km},\\,JL=450\\,\\text{km}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL-SL & <JS<JL+SL \\\\ 450-150 & <JS<450+150 \\\\ 300 & <JS<600.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance from Captain Sparrow's to Captain Jones' ship must be *between $300\\,\\text{km}$ and $600\\,\\text{km}$*.",
